[x] Seed the root objective on init and report charters without one =root-objective $Per Decision 43: init writes an objective file and links the root charter to it, once, as it seeds the root alias; doctor reports any charter with no objective, never refusing to load. Spec text in workspace.md and objectives.md, implementation in Core and the CLI. Existing objective files mostly lack frontmatter ids: give them ids in the same pass, as the old charter map asked. DONE 2026-10-02: objectives/README.md is the root objective (spec adabbf1); init creates it and links it from a root charter it creates; doctor warns on live charters with no objective (core 92741d7). This workspace: 11 charters flagged, including the root; its root objective is the human's to write.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T20:22 %2026-10-01T22:44 <019f5895-6948-70b2-88e2-a7685b0dddda #01a0faa2-1806-7129-814b-f5efdcd6ff41
[x] Add a batch locate command to the CLI =cli-locate $Per Decision 44: queries return identities, and the CLI answers where each is stored. Read ids on stdin (the output of any --format ids command) or as arguments, and print one JSON record per id with its file, line and charter root, in one process, so a 50-row list costs one call. Unknown ids are reported, not fatal. CLI documentation, not the spec: storage is the implementation's business. DONE 2026-10-02: clearhead locate (core, Add clearhead locate), JSON array in input order, reuses workspace_snapshot; documented in the CLI README. Objectives are not located yet: the domain Objective carries no file path.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T22:07 %2026-10-01T23:57 #01a0fb02-e644-7317-88ad-1e76ecfb2b0e
[x] Decide the query surface\: raw V5 or a derived view =query-surface-spike $Decision 43 settled what the graph means, not what people write queries against. Rewrite three real queries (for-human, index agenda, index unscheduled) twice against the spec fixture's expected.ttl: once on raw V5, once on a sketched convenience view generated from V5 by a CONSTRUCT (a data shape, spec-owned like the JSON schemas, adding no meaning). Run both, compare with the human, then decide whether the view earns its place; v5-projection and v5-queries wait on this. RESULTS 2026-10-02 (spike files in the session scratchpad: fixture copy with groceries Active and the fence tagged +human; v4 export; V5 graph; raw and view queries; run.py). Same answers three ways for all three queries. Lines without comments or prefixes, v4 / raw V5 / view: for-human 12/27/6, unscheduled 48/55/17, agenda 50/53/17. The view is one 56-line CONSTRUCT and yields 53 triples from 178. Raw V5 is barely longer than v4 for the index queries (v4 is already heavy) but unreadable: opaque IRIs, the closed check repeated three times. The view needs one derived status per action and plain names for paths, nothing more. Found: scheduled and due are indistinguishable in the mapping (separate action); times as written make a date and a datetime incomparable, so both raw and view compare date prefixes as strings. Waiting on the human's decision. DECIDED 2026-10-02: Decision 45. The application vocabulary is the graph people query and export; the ontology is its mapping to CCO.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:24 %2026-10-02T00:35 #01a0fb80-65bd-77bd-b573-05bf804556fc
[_] Resolve locations in nvim through clearhead locate =nvim-locate $Per Decision 44, query rows will carry identities, not locations. view.lua (quickfix: charter_root, source_file, source_line) and tree_view.lua (source_file, source_line) must take the row ids, call clearhead locate once for the whole list, and build entries from its answer. Land together with v5-queries so the plugin never sees rows without a way to locate them. CANCELLED 2026-10-02: Decision 45 puts file and line in the application graph, so query rows keep their locations and nvim needs no change. clearhead locate stays as a convenience.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T22:07 %2026-10-02T00:35 <01a0fb02-e644-7317-88ad-1e76ecfb2b0e #01a0fb02-e66c-74e2-bb7d-bd6b4fb04e2a
[x] Name the application vocabulary $FOR THE HUMAN. Decision 45 wants a fresh name for the vocabulary people query. Recommended: the ClearHead application vocabulary, namespace https://clearhead.us/vocab/app/v1# with prefix app:, absorbing the retired ws: terms (file and line now belong to it). It reads as what it is, an application layer, and the v1 leaves room for a v2. Alternatives: https://clearhead.us/vocab/clearhead/v1# with prefix clearhead: (plainer, but sounds like the meaning layer, which is CCO's job); or a product word of your choosing. app-vocabulary waits on this. ANSWERED 2026-10-02 by the human: app, https://clearhead.us/vocab/app/v1#, since we are still in ClearHead; on condition that the path from the application vocabulary to the domain ontology stays clean (see app-to-cco).$ !1 +human ^2026-10-02T00:35 %2026-10-02T10:41 #01a0fb8a-37d6-74f8-9b7f-2558dfe00000
[x] Pick the duration sigil $FOR THE HUMAN. Decision 46 frees duration from the date, but D cannot be a sigil: sigil characters are excluded from titles (tree-sitter-actions/patterns.js METADATA_CHARS), so D would ban the letter D from every title (37 of 303 unique titles across ~/Products contain one). Any new sigil costs the same for its own character, escapable with a backslash. Counted in the same titles: & 0, | 1 (inside a link, which the grammar parses separately), ; 2 (both old RRULE fixtures), { 0, backtick 0, ? 3, ( 4. Recommended: | (for example |30, minutes), rare in prose titles and already special inside links. Alternatives: ; (rare, but ordinary English punctuation), & (zero here, but R&D and Q&A are common titles in general). ANSWERED 2026-10-02 by the human: | (|30, minutes).$ !1 +human ^2026-10-02T12:13 %2026-10-02T12:16 #01a0fe08-fd5e-7315-9019-8974bb322e24
[x] Distinguish scheduled from due in the mapping =scheduled-vs-due $Found by the query-surface spike, 2026-10-02: a scheduled time (not before) and a due time (by) both project as a condition whose description carries a datetime on its bearer, so the graph cannot tell a start from a deadline; only the UUIDv5 role, which is a hash, differs. Neither the shapes nor the reasoner caught it, since both are valid and consistent. Find standard terms that say which bound a time is (for example a description of a temporal interval that the time starts or finishes, CCO interval starts / interval finishes, or OWL-Time), update ontology.md, the fixture, the shapes, and add an invalid graph or a query to the gate that must recover the difference. Also decide how a date and a datetime compare, since times are now as written. PLAN 2026-10-02 (for the next agent; ask before deviating from the recommendation). RECOMMENDED: one window condition per action, as the DSL spec already words it (action_file_format.md: '@' and ':' are the lower and upper bounds of one inclusive window). The Performance Specification's description describes a BFO temporal interval (obo:BFO_0000202), the window; '@' is its first instant (obo:BFO_0000222 has first instant) and ':' its last (obo:BFO_0000224 has last instant), each a BFO temporal instant (obo:BFO_0000203) designated (cco:ont00001916) by a CCO Temporal Instant Identifier (cco:ont00000540) whose bearer has the datetime (cco:ont00001767) or date (cco:ont00001771) as written. All BFO/CCO, no new vocabulary; about 20 triples for a two-bound window, acceptable now that Decision 45 makes CCO the meaning layer, not the queried one. ALTERNATIVE: W3C OWL-Time (time:Interval, time:hasBeginning, time:hasEnd, time:inXSDDateTimeStamp): lighter, but a second upper vocabulary beside BFO; reject unless the BFO form fails HermiT. STEPS: 1) ontology: add a window example (an action with both bounds, one with only a due date) to v5/examples, extend cq3 or add cq9 'what is due by a date' that must tell start from deadline, add its expected CSV, make -C ontology/v5 test green. 2) spec ontology.md: replace the scheduledDateTime/dueDateTime rows and the when/scheduled, when/due roles with when/window, when/window/start, when/window/end (and their identifier and bearer roles). 3) fixture: regenerate expected.ttl with docs/spikes/2026-10-02-query-surface/gen_expected.py (update its condition helper), then extend graph.shapes.ttl (a window has at most one first and one last instant; a time-bearing description must describe a window) and add invalid graphs for each new rule. 4) scripts/check-graph-shapes.py and validate-pinned green, then commit spec + ontology + platform. OPEN, settle with app-vocabulary: how a date and a datetime compare once times are as written (the spike compared date prefixes as strings); the application vocabulary may expose a normalized comparable value beside the written one. RESCOPED 2026-10-02 with the human: this action is now step 1 only (prove the window pattern in ontology/v5 examples and a competency query that must tell start from deadline, HermiT green), which settles the meaning before app-vocabulary. Steps 2 and 3 move into app-to-cco: the mapping CONSTRUCT emits the window and generates expected.ttl, so nothing here rewrites ontology.md's role table or regenerates expected.ttl with gen_expected.py, both of which the later actions replace. WIDENED 2026-10-02 with the human: step 1 is now a small time-model spike, since time, date, duration and recurrence are one small, critical part of the architecture. Duration is settled (Decision 46). Still open, to settle by the spike, not by argument: a date-only bound is a whole day (from its start for @, to its end for :); floating versus offset times compared at query time in the viewer's zone, not baked into the graph; the window pattern in CCO. Method: about ten awkward lines (date-only due, floating beside offset, D with and without @, a VEVENT occurrence, a recurring VTODO), three real queries (today's agenda, overdue, what fits in 30 minutes now), two or three candidate app: shapes; record the one that answers them plainly and correctly, with the CCO window as its meaning. SETTLED 2026-10-02 (Decision 47, human): a date-only bound is the whole day, for @ and : alike, as calendars treat all-day events. The spike now tests that rule and the open zone question (floating versus offset, compared in the viewer's zone at query time), and writes both into action_file_format.md's bound semantics. STEP 1 DONE 2026-10-02 as the time-model spike (docs/history/time-model-spike): S2 accepted by the human (Decision 47): written value plus derived instants notBefore and lateFrom (exclusive), resolved in the viewer's zone at projection. Remaining CCO work (window first instant = notBefore; the upper end needs care, since BFO has-last-instant is inclusive) moves into app-to-cco with the earlier steps 2 and 3.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:28 %2026-10-02T14:28 #01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d
[x] Define the application vocabulary in the spec =app-vocabulary $Per Decision 45: a fresh-named ClearHead application vocabulary, owned by the specification beside the JSON schemas. Start from the spike's view and v4, but complete: every DSL, charter and objective field and state, plus file and line (optional for another backend), so the CCO graph can be generated from it. Rewrite specifications/ontology.md around it (the application graph first; CCO as its meaning, mapped in the ontology repo), add its SHACL shapes, and add expected-app.ttl to the graph fixture: the workspace's exact application graph. The name waits on the human. Starting material: docs/history/query-surface-spike/view/view.rq (the sketch, a subset: add descriptions, created and completed times, objectives, metrics, aliases, charter parents, plans, file and line). NAMED 2026-10-02: prefix app:, namespace https://clearhead.us/vocab/app/v1#, absorbing ws:. From the time-model spike (Decision 47): bounds carry the written value and derived instants (notBefore, lateFrom). Recommended, not yet decided: emit effective bounds inherited from ancestors as derived terms too, since the spec says queries use them and an ancestor walk with aggregates in every query repeats the S1 problem. Derived terms rule: computed from files plus viewer context, never written back, mapped to CCO. Leave room for event-triggers: a wait (<) targets an action or a calendar event, and an event's time feeds notBefore. Option A taken (human did not object, 2026-10-02): notBefore and lateFrom are the effective window, combining own bounds, ancestors' bounds and waited-on events; app:start and app:due stay as written on the line. PROGRESS 2026-10-02: draft vocabulary agreed with the human (classes Action, Charter, Objective, Context, Metric; rdfs:label, dcterms:description/created, skos:broader reused; one app:state with IRI values; app:partOf points up; app:serves; written app:start/due/closed plus derived notBefore/lateFrom/waitsOn; app:file relative to the data root, no root triple, no Workspace node: the named graph is the workspace). '%' is app:closed, one date for completed or cancelled. expected-app.ttl in the spec fixture (spec 219d996) with inheritance cases; docs/history/app-vocabulary-check shows the spike's queries at a quarter of v4's length with v4's answers. Remaining: ontology.md app section, app shapes with invalid graphs, wire into check-graph-shapes.py; metrics and recurrence with v5-fixture-gaps. DONE 2026-10-02 (spec 53e9b35): ontology.md leads with the Application Graph (rules, terms, state values, effective window and waits; CCO under 'Meaning' until app-to-cco moves it); schemas/app.shapes.ttl with 17 invalid-app graphs, each failing on exactly its shape and constraint; check-graph-shapes.py runs both suites. Left to v5-fixture-gaps: recurrence and objective states in the app graph.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 %2026-10-02T15:11 <01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d #01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5
[x] Map the application vocabulary to CCO in the ontology =app-to-cco $Per Decision 45 the ontology becomes the mapping: one SPARQL CONSTRUCT from the application vocabulary to CCO v2.2 and IAO, in the ontology repo, defining what each application term means. Helper-node IRIs must be computable inside SPARQL (no UUIDv5 there), for example a hash of owner and role; update the spec's Helper node IRIs and expected.ttl to match. Gate: the mapping of the fixture's expected-app.ttl is isomorphic to expected.ttl, which passes the CCO shapes, HermiT and the ontology's verify rules; wire it into validate-pinned. Consider rewriting the ontology's examples in the application vocabulary and mapping them for reasoning. ADDED 2026-10-02 (the human's condition on the name: a clean path from app to the domain ontology): the gate also fails if any term in the application vocabulary is not used by the mapping, so no app term exists without a meaning. Includes scheduled-vs-due steps 2 and 3: the window (first and last instant) is emitted by the mapping, the spec's helper-node roles and the shapes follow it, with an invalid graph per new rule. OPEN 2026-10-02: expected.ttl states only each action's own bounds and waits; app:notBefore, app:lateFrom and app:waitsOn include inherited ones. Decide whether the mapping emits a CCO condition per effective wait and window (true, but redundant with the ancestor's) or maps only the written terms and defines the derived ones as derivations; the clean-path gate needs every app term covered either way. RECOMMENDED 2026-10-02 (start here next session; not yet confirmed by the human): derived terms map to real CCO conditions, since under Decision 45 an unmapped term has no meaning, and an inherited wait or window is true of the child. A wait condition's IRI derives from its target, so app:after and app:waitsOn yield the same node and do not duplicate. Each action's CCO window (BFO temporal interval, first instant = notBefore; upper end needs care, as has-last-instant is inclusive and lateFrom is not) comes from the effective instants, which also fixes scheduled-vs-due. Cost: expected.ttl grows by children's inherited conditions. CONFIRMED 2026-10-02 by the human: derived terms map to CCO. A sub-action of a waiting action is itself waiting; the hierarchy is the reason. The CONSTRUCT materializes inherited conditions, because a CCO-only consumer cannot derive them down has-part without rules. Still open: the window's upper end (has-last-instant is inclusive, lateFrom is not). AMENDED 2026-10-03 by Decisions 49 and 50: helper nodes are blank nodes (the CONSTRUCT mints them natively; fixtures compare by isomorphism, RDFC-1.0 for byte-stable exports), so the helper-IRI step above is gone. Write the mapping in specifications/ontology/ from the start, not in the ontology repo. The every-term-is-mapped check takes its term list from app.shapes.ttl (every sh:path and sh:targetClass); no separate app.ttl. DONE 2026-10-03: specifications/ontology/mapping/ (15 queries) maps expected-app.ttl to exactly expected.ttl; graph shapes, HermiT and verify pass; scripts/check-graph-shapes.py enforces the mapping and term coverage (unmapped.ttl lists the deliberate exceptions) in validate-pinned. Decided with the human: CCO carries the effective conditions (waitsOn, notBefore, lateFrom), not the as-written terms; @ is a Prescriptive ICE part of the action (not an act, keeping ontology Decision 4); a time condition carries the text not before or late from; a metric's review_date is removed. Rewriting the ontology's own examples in the application vocabulary is left for fold-ontology.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 %2026-10-03T00:22 <01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5 #01a0fb89-fdcd-722f-a0ab-5399b7a3c0cb
[_] Keep do-dates' written precision =do-bound $Decision 48 follow-up. scheduled_at is still a DateTime<Local>, and the writer emits every @ as YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM, so @2026-10-05 comes back as @2026-10-05T00:00 on any CLI write (the bug Due fixed for :). Move it to domain::time::Bound; W015 needs @'s precision, and Decision 47 places a date-only @ as an all-day event. Small: about two uses outside calendar sync. Cancelled 2026-10-03: folded into intent-range, whose Core step makes scheduled_at the interval type built on Bound.$ !3 ^2026-10-02T18:37 %2026-10-03T10:21 #01a0ff69-2150-78e1-aaaa-e880b316e19b
